### Health check
GET http://localhost:8080/api/health


### Register a user (for testing)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/register
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "name": "V1 Tester",
  "email": "v1tester@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}


### Login as that user
POST http://localhost:8080/api/auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "email": "v1tester@example.com",
  "password": "password123"
}


### 1. /api/v1/tickets rejects requests without a token -> expect 401
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tickets


### 2. /api/v1/tickets works with a valid token -> expect 200
# Replace TOKEN below with the token from the login request above
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tickets
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_USER_TOKEN


### 3. Old /api/tickets endpoint still works -> expect 200
GET http://localhost:8080/api/tickets
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_USER_TOKEN


### POST /api/v1/tickets with USER token -> expect 201 (v1 relaxes this rule)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/v1/tickets
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_USER_TOKEN
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Laptop is slow",
  "description": "Laptop takes long to boot.",
  "category": "Hardware",
  "priority": "MEDIUM",
  "createdBy": "v1tester@example.com"
}


### POST /api/tickets (old) with USER token -> expect 403 (old rule unchanged, ADMIN only)
POST http://localhost:8080/api/tickets
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_USER_TOKEN
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "title": "Laptop is slow",
  "description": "Laptop takes long to boot.",
  "category": "Hardware",
  "priority": "MEDIUM",
  "createdBy": "v1tester@example.com"
}


### Ticket report by status, without token -> expect 401
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/reports/tickets-by-status


### Ticket report by status, with token -> expect 200 + grouped counts
GET http://localhost:8080/api/v1/reports/tickets-by-status
Authorization: Bearer REPLACE_WITH_USER_TOKEN
